taType=dailyRevenue","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-08-03"},{"id":"stellar-volume","title":"Stellar DEX volume API","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://api.llama.fi/overview/dexs/Stellar?dataType=dailyVolume","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-08-03"},{"id":"stablecoin-chains","title":"Stablecoin supply by chain API","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://stablecoins.llama.fi/stablecoinchains","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-08-03"}],"forensic_analysis":{"version":"forensic-analysis-v1","observation_snapshot":{"observed_at":"2026-08-03T17:20:28.274Z","provider":"DefiLlama","method":"Single sequential API run using the latest historicalChainTvl point, provider-reported 30-day fee/revenue/DEX aggregates, and current stablecoinchains values. The historical TVL point is dated 2026-08-03 and can be revised after this retrieval time.","historical_tvl_point_date":"2026-08-03","latest_tvl_usd":214161726,"peak_tvl_usd":243985238,"peak_tvl_date":"2026-06-19","tvl_drawdown_pct":12.22,"stablecoin_supply":{"pegged_usd":890715813.3151151,"pegged_eur":21905896.902476225,"pegged_jpy":599715.3723063797,"pegged_chf":8971.037728313426,"pegged_aud":4620157.298629123},"dex_volume_30d_usd":35387627,"fees_30d_usd":2929905.94,"revenue_30d_usd":226656.94,"observation_completed_at":"2026-08-03T17:20:30.954Z"},"outcome":{"label":"successful","summary":"Stellar is a successful specialist settlement network, not a broad smart-contract winner. In the DefiLlama API snapshot fetched at 2026-08-03T17:20:28.274Z, the latest 2026-08-03 historical point was $214.2 million of DeFi TVL, with roughly $890.7 million of dollar stablecoins, $35.4 million of 30-day DEX volume, $2.93 million of 30-day fees, and $226,657 of 30-day revenue. Those observations show a live payment and issuance base 12.2% below its TVL peak, while the much smaller DEX and revenue figures keep the outcome below a claim of category-wide dominance. These figures are a point-in-time snapshot.","confidence":"high","as_of":"2026-08-03","source_refs":["stellar-tvl","stellar-fees","stellar-revenue","stellar-volume","stablecoin-chains"]},"why":{"summary":"Observed: Stellar retains a large stablecoin base, TVL close to its June 2026 peak, and recurring fees, while its DEX activity and retained revenue are much smaller. Documented design: it made issued assets, trustlines, payment operations, anchors, low XLM resource requirements, and safety-first federated consensus first-class long before adding general smart contracts. Strategic inference: that sequencing created a durable distribution niche around payments and regulated issuance without requiring Stellar to win the EVM liquidity race. Adding Rust/Wasm contracts later lets those classic assets enter programmable workflows without discarding their issuer controls. The trade-off is structural: issuer and anchor dependence remains offchain, SCP quorum choices can stop liveness, and strong stablecoin settlement does not automatically create large XLM or protocol-revenue capture.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["stellar-scp","stellar-assets","stellar-contracts","stellar-lumens","stellar-halt","stellar-tvl","stellar-fees","stellar-revenue","stablecoin-chains"]},"strategic_choices":[{"decision":"Use federated Byzantine agreement with validator-selected quorum sets instead of proof of work or token-weighted proof of stake.","consequence":"Stellar avoids mining and validator-reward economics and prioritizes safety over liveness, but operators must configure intersecting quorums correctly; the 2019 halt demonstrated that configuration failures can stop the network even when safety holds.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["stellar-scp","stellar-halt"]},{"decision":"Build native asset issuance, trustlines, exchange, and anchor standards into the payment rail before offering general smart contracts.","consequence":"Issuers and payment businesses received lower-complexity primitives and redemption integrations, helping Stellar retain a stablecoin niche; users still depend on each issuer or anchor for reserves, authorization, and offchain redemption.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["stellar-assets","stablecoin-chains"]},{"decision":"Add Soroban Rust/Wasm contracts through a later protocol rollout and make classic assets accessible through the Stellar Asset Contract.","consequence":"Stellar can extend a mature payment ledger into programmable finance without replacing its existing asset system, while inheriting a newer contract-state and storage failure surface illustrated by the archival incident.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["stellar-contracts","stellar-assets","stellar-archival"]},{"decision":"Keep XLM focused on fees, rent, and minimum balances rather than making every issued asset settle economically through XLM.","consequence":"Applications can move dollar and other issuer assets cheaply without taking native-token price risk, but payment or stablecoin growth need not translate proportionally into XLM demand or protocol revenue.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["stellar-lumens","stellar-fees","stellar-revenue","stablecoin-chains"]}],"counterfactual":{"summary":"Launching EVM-compatible general contracts earlier might have attracted more portable DeFi liquidity and developers, but it would also have exposed the young payment network to a larger application, state, and exploit surface before its issuance rails matured. Retaining only classic payment operations would have reduced Soroban-specific complexity but capped programmability. The reviewed sources do not establish that either path would have produced more durable usage, and the 2025 archival failure shows the real cost of the added state machinery rather than proving the expansion was a mistake.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["stellar-assets","stellar-contracts","stellar-archival","stellar-tvl","stellar-volume"]},"watch":[{"signal":"Stablecoin supply, TVL, DEX volume, fees, and retained revenue tracked separately.","implication":"Growth across settlement and revenue would show that payment distribution is converting into a broader economy; stablecoins rising without fees or revenue would preserve the value-capture gap.","source_refs":["stablecoin-chains","stellar-tvl","stellar-volume","stellar-fees","stellar-revenue"]},{"signal":"Independent quorum-set, Tier-1 validator, issuer, and anchor concentration measurements plus any new liveness incident.","implication":"More independent control would strengthen the safety-and-resilience thesis; concentrated trust or another configuration halt would weaken it.","source_refs":["stellar-scp","stellar-halt"]},{"signal":"Soroban application usage and state-integrity incidents after the archival fixes.","implication":"Sustained contract use without repeated state failures supports the late-programmability strategy; recurring corruption would show that expansion added more risk than durable demand.","source_refs":["stellar-contracts","stellar-archival","stellar-tvl","stellar-fees"]}],"unknowns":[{"question":"Who ultimately controls the quorum sets and infrastructure relied upon by the largest validators?","resolution_trigger":"A current independent entity-level quorum and hosting analysis."},{"question":"How concentrated are stablecoin supply, payment volume, and redemption capacity by issuer and anchor?","resolution_trigger":"Issuer-level balances, flows, redemption terms, and independently verified reserve coverage."},{"question":"What share of Soroban usage persists without grants or temporary incentives?","resolution_trigger":"Public application cohorts, fee attribution, and incentive-adjusted retention."},{"question":"How much network growth creates incremental XLM demand rather than only demand for issued assets?","resolution_trigger":"A reproducible decomposition of fees, rent, minimum balances, circulating float, and application asset demand."}],"review":{"status":"current","last_reviewed_at":"2026-08-03","next_review_at":"2026-08-10","reviewer":"chaindump-research-desk"}}}')
