lume","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-08-03"},{"id":"stablecoin-chains","title":"Stablecoin supply by chain API","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://stablecoins.llama.fi/stablecoinchains","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-08-03"}],"forensic_analysis":{"version":"forensic-analysis-v1","observation_snapshot":{"observed_at":"2026-08-03T17:20:28.274Z","provider":"DefiLlama","method":"Single sequential API run using the latest historicalChainTvl point, provider-reported 30-day fee/revenue/DEX aggregates, and current stablecoinchains values. The historical TVL point is dated 2026-08-03 and can be revised after this retrieval time.","historical_tvl_point_date":"2026-08-03","latest_tvl_usd":417197859,"peak_tvl_usd":2636094260,"peak_tvl_date":"2025-10-09","tvl_drawdown_pct":84.17,"stablecoin_supply":{"pegged_usd":432030086.3603716},"dex_volume_30d_usd":791925163.36,"fees_30d_usd":4857036.13,"revenue_30d_usd":710635.54,"observation_completed_at":"2026-08-03T17:20:30.954Z"},"outcome":{"label":"middling","summary":"Sui is a technically differentiated but middling L1. In the DefiLlama API snapshot fetched at 2026-08-03T17:20:28.274Z, the latest 2026-08-03 historical point was $417.2 million TVL, 84.2% below its October 2025 peak, alongside roughly $432.0 million of dollar stablecoins, $791.9 million of 30-day DEX volume, $4.86 million of 30-day fees, and $710,636 of 30-day revenue. That is meaningful current activity, but the deep capital drawdown and four officially documented halt or stall incidents across January and May 2026 prevent a stronger outcome call. These figures are a point-in-time snapshot.","confidence":"high","as_of":"2026-08-03","source_refs":["sui-tvl","sui-fees","sui-revenue","sui-volume","stablecoin-chains","sui-stall-2026","sui-halts-2026"]},"why":{"summary":"Observed: Sui still processes substantial DEX volume and fees, but TVL is more than 84% below peak and official reports document a January stall and three May halt incidents. Documented design: Sui rejected EVM compatibility for an object-centric Move model, distinguished owned objects from shared objects, and built first-party consumer, liquidity, storage, wallet, and developer infrastructure around that architecture. Strategic inference: this gives asset-heavy applications a coherent concurrency and ownership model that competitors cannot reproduce with a thin compatibility layer, but it also makes Sui responsible for a novel stack whose correctness and distribution must mature together. Foundation-managed token releases and vertical product support accelerate bootstrapping, yet obscure how much liquidity and usage are independent of allocations, incentives, and first-party infrastructure.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["sui-objects","sui-move","sui-token-schedule","sui-2026-strategy","sui-stall-2026","sui-halts-2026","sui-tvl","sui-fees","sui-volume"]},"strategic_choices":[{"decision":"Reject EVM compatibility and make Sui Move plus object ownership the execution model.","consequence":"Owned objects can support direct, asset-oriented execution and stronger language-level constraints, while developers and liquidity cannot migrate through ordinary EVM tooling and the novel model must earn its own ecosystem.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["sui-objects","sui-move"]},{"decision":"Separate owned-object transactions from shared-object consensus to pursue low latency and parallel execution.","consequence":"The architecture can avoid unnecessary coordination for independent assets, but interactions touching shared state still require consensus and the larger set of execution paths increases implementation and restart-state complexity.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["sui-objects","sui-stall-2026","sui-halts-2026"]},{"decision":"Vertically support liquidity, wallet, storage, gaming, and consumer infrastructure instead of limiting the original contributor and Foundation to the base protocol.","consequence":"Integrated products can reduce cold-start friction and make the differentiated object model usable, while first-party breadth creates neutrality, dependency, and attribution questions when ecosystem activity is evaluated.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["sui-2026-strategy","sui-volume","sui-fees"]},{"decision":"Release most of the capped 10 billion SUI supply over time through Foundation-managed schedules and ecosystem programs.","consequence":"The reserve can fund validators, builders, and distribution, but the schedule is explicitly adjustable to network needs, leaving future float, incentive dependence, and Foundation allocation power material to the outcome.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["sui-token-schedule"]}],"counterfactual":{"summary":"An EVM-compatible path would have lowered developer and liquidity switching costs, but it would have surrendered much of the object-ownership differentiation Sui was created to test. A narrower base-protocol role might reduce first-party dependency, while slowing the wallets, liquidity, storage, and consumer products needed to demonstrate the architecture. More conservative upgrade sequencing or stronger failure containment could plausibly have reduced 2026 liveness incidents, but the postmortems do not quantify what delivery speed or functionality would have been sacrificed.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["sui-objects","sui-move","sui-2026-strategy","sui-stall-2026","sui-halts-2026"]},"watch":[{"signal":"TVL, stablecoins, DEX volume, fees, and revenue after Foundation and application incentives are separated where possible.","implication":"Capital and revenue retention without subsidies would support a durable object-model economy; volume without recovering capital or revenue would keep the chain middling.","source_refs":["sui-tvl","stablecoin-chains","sui-volume","sui-fees","sui-revenue"]},{"signal":"Frequency, duration, and blast radius of future liveness incidents after the May 2026 containment work.","implication":"A long incident-free period would show the novel stack is maturing; another input- or restart-triggered halt would weaken the reliability thesis.","source_refs":["sui-stall-2026","sui-halts-2026"]},{"signal":"Actual circulating supply, Foundation deployments, validator stake concentration, and adjustments to the published release path.","implication":"Transparent, broadly distributed releases reduce overhang and control risk; discretionary or concentrated deployment preserves both.","source_refs":["sui-token-schedule"]}],"unknowns":[{"question":"How much Sui liquidity and user retention survives after grants, emissions, and trading incentives are removed?","resolution_trigger":"Incentive-adjusted application cohorts, liquidity, volume, fees, and revenue."},{"question":"Who beneficially controls validator stake and Foundation delegations?","resolution_trigger":"A current independent entity-level stake and operator map."},{"question":"How much activity depends on Mysten- or Foundation-supported products and infrastructure?","resolution_trigger":"Attributable usage and revenue by independent versus affiliated applications and services."},{"question":"Will post-May failure containment prevent a single malformed input or restart-state bug from halting the network again?","resolution_trigger":"Published remediation verification plus sustained production evidence or a contrary incident."}],"review":{"status":"current","last_reviewed_at":"2026-08-03","next_review_at":"2026-08-10","reviewer":"chaindump-research-desk"}}}')
