DEX volume API","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://api.llama.fi/overview/dexs/Algorand?dataType=dailyVolume","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-08-03"},{"id":"stablecoin-chains","title":"Stablecoin supply by chain API","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://stablecoins.llama.fi/stablecoinchains","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-08-03"}],"forensic_analysis":{"version":"forensic-analysis-v1","observation_snapshot":{"observed_at":"2026-08-03T17:20:28.274Z","provider":"DefiLlama","method":"Single sequential API run using the latest historicalChainTvl point, provider-reported 30-day fee/revenue/DEX aggregates, and current stablecoinchains values. The historical TVL point is dated 2026-08-03 and can be revised after this retrieval time.","historical_tvl_point_date":"2026-08-03","latest_tvl_usd":29658178,"peak_tvl_usd":319103080,"peak_tvl_date":"2024-02-10","tvl_drawdown_pct":90.71,"stablecoin_supply":{"pegged_usd":37025435.04231672,"pegged_eur":845833.6039442576},"dex_volume_30d_usd":7264711,"fees_30d_usd":9464.9,"revenue_30d_usd":3916.9,"observation_completed_at":"2026-08-03T17:20:30.954Z"},"outcome":{"label":"declining","summary":"Algorand is a declining but operational L1. In the DefiLlama API snapshot fetched at 2026-08-03T17:20:28.274Z, the latest 2026-08-03 historical point was $29.7 million TVL, 90.7% below its February 2024 peak, alongside roughly $37.0 million of dollar stablecoins plus €0.85 million of euro stablecoins, $7.26 million of 30-day DEX volume, $9,465 of 30-day fees, and $3,917 of 30-day revenue. Those figures show a functioning residual economy, but not a self-reinforcing capital, application, or fee base comparable with the network''s earlier peak. These figures are a point-in-time snapshot.","confidence":"high","as_of":"2026-08-03","source_refs":["algo-tvl","algo-fees","algo-revenue","algo-volume","stablecoin-chains"]},"why":{"summary":"Observed: Algorand remains live with stablecoins and DEX activity, but TVL is more than 90% below peak and monthly fees and revenue are very small. Documented design: cryptographic sortition, fast finality, native protocol primitives, a capped ALGO supply, and low resource requirements produced a technically coherent chain. Strategic inference: those properties did not create the developer, liquidity, and application distribution loop needed to defend share against EVM and other high-throughput ecosystems. Extremely low fees improve user economics but leave little fee-funded security or token capture; Foundation-funded governance and ecosystem incentives supported participation without proving independent demand. The 2025 shift from governance rewards to active proposer rewards and xGov is a rational correction toward security and accountable grants, but current market data does not yet show a durable economic reversal.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["algo-consensus","algo-staking","algo-token","algo-governance-shift","algo-xgov","algo-transparency-q1-2026","algo-tvl","algo-fees","algo-revenue","algo-volume"]},"strategic_choices":[{"decision":"Use cryptographic sortition to select stake-weighted proposers and committees rather than a persistent fixed validator set.","consequence":"Consensus roles are difficult to target before selection and participation can run on modest hardware, while influence still follows online stake and must be measured at the beneficial-owner and infrastructure levels.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["algo-consensus"]},{"decision":"Build bespoke Layer-1 asset, atomic-transfer, and AVM primitives instead of adopting EVM compatibility as the main distribution strategy.","consequence":"Algorand can optimize finality and native operations as one stack, but developers, applications, and liquidity cannot migrate through the dominant EVM toolchain without translation and new ecosystem commitments.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["algo-consensus","algo-tvl","algo-volume"]},{"decision":"Keep transaction fees extremely low and subsidize ecosystem participation and rewards from allocated ALGO pools.","consequence":"Users receive cheap execution and the Foundation can bootstrap specific programs, but current fee and revenue totals are too small to demonstrate a self-funding security and application economy independent of finite allocations.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["algo-token","algo-staking","algo-transparency-q1-2026","algo-fees","algo-revenue"]},{"decision":"End rewarded quarterly governance, redirect incentives to active block proposers, and move builder funding toward xGov tied to consensus participation.","consequence":"Rewards align more directly with network security and grant voting can become more accountable, while decaying bonuses, eligibility thresholds, and residual Foundation-operated xGov components remain material dependencies.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["algo-staking","algo-governance-shift","algo-xgov"]}],"counterfactual":{"summary":"Earlier EVM compatibility might have reduced switching costs and improved access to existing applications and liquidity, but could also have diluted the tightly integrated protocol and AVM design; the sources do not prove the net result. Paying active block proposers earlier instead of rewarding passive holders and periodic governors might have strengthened operator incentives sooner. Higher fees could improve direct economic capture while compromising Algorand''s low-cost proposition. None of these changes alone would guarantee product distribution, and treating technical quality as sufficient was the more important strategic error.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["algo-consensus","algo-staking","algo-governance-shift","algo-tvl","algo-fees","algo-volume"]},"watch":[{"signal":"TVL, stablecoins, DEX volume, fees, and revenue after the shift from governance rewards to staking rewards.","implication":"Sustained growth without renewed broad subsidies would support recovery; flat or falling activity would confirm that the incentive redesign improved security mechanics without restoring demand.","source_refs":["algo-governance-shift","algo-tvl","stablecoin-chains","algo-volume","algo-fees","algo-revenue"]},{"signal":"Online stake, proposer, relay, hosting, and beneficial-owner concentration under the active reward model.","implication":"More independent proposers and infrastructure would validate the decentralization goal; concentration or reward capture by a small set would weaken it.","source_refs":["algo-consensus","algo-staking"]},{"signal":"xGov participation, grant delivery, Foundation-operated components, treasury runway, and follow-on application usage.","implication":"Transparent grants that produce recurring users and fees would support a recovery path; governance activity without product outcomes would not.","source_refs":["algo-xgov","algo-transparency-q1-2026","algo-fees","algo-volume"]}],"unknowns":[{"question":"Who beneficially controls online stake, block proposals, relays, and the infrastructure behind them?","resolution_trigger":"A current independent entity-level consensus and hosting map."},{"question":"Will fee income and decaying proposer bonuses sustain enough independent operators over the long run?","resolution_trigger":"A multi-period model reconciled to onchain fees, rewards, operator costs, and participation."},{"question":"How much remaining stablecoin and application activity depends on Foundation incentives or a few counterparties?","resolution_trigger":"Protocol- and counterparty-level concentration with incentives separated."},{"question":"Can xGov-funded public goods create recurring application demand rather than only governance participation?","resolution_trigger":"Milestone-level grant outcomes linked to retained users, volume, fees, and independent maintenance."}],"review":{"status":"current","last_reviewed_at":"2026-08-03","next_review_at":"2026-08-10","reviewer":"chaindump-research-desk"}}}')
)
UPDATE chain_facts AS facts
SET
  data = CASE facts.dimension
    WHEN 'synthesis' THEN json_set(
      facts.data,
      '$.forensic_analysis',
      json(json_extract((SELECT payload FROM causal_seed), '$.forensic_analysis'))
    )
    WHEN '_meta' THEN json_set(
      facts.data,
      '$.forensic_analysis_version', 'forensic-analysis-v1',
      '$.last_reviewed', '2026-08-03',
      '$.next_review_at',
      json_extract(
        (SELECT payload FROM causal_seed),
        '$.forensic_analysis.review.next_review_at'
      )
    )
    ELSE facts.data
  END
  ,
  sources = CASE
    WHEN facts.dimension = 'synthesis' THEN (
      SELECT json_group_array(json(source_json))
      FROM (
        SELECT source_json
        FROM (
          SELECT
            CASE
              WHEN new_source.value IS NULL THEN old_source.value
              ELSE json_set(
                json_patch(old_source.value, new_source.value),
                '$.checked_at',
                CASE
                  WHEN json_extract(old_source.value, '$.checked_at')
                    > json_extract(new_source.value, '$.checked_at')
                    THEN json_extract(old_source.value, '$.checked_at')
                  ELSE COALESCE(
                    json_extract(new_source.value, '$.checked_at'),
                    json_extract(old_source.value, '$.checked_at')
                  )
                END
              )
            END AS source_json,
            old_source.key AS position
          FROM json_each(COALESCE(facts.sources, '[]')) AS old_source
          LEFT JOIN json_each(
            json_extract((SELECT payload FROM causal_seed), '$.sources')
          ) AS new_source
            ON json_extract(new_source.value, '$.url')
              = json_extract(old_source.value, '$.url')
          WHERE json_extract(old_source.value, '$.url') IS NULL
            OR old_source.key = (
              SELECT MIN(candidate.key)
              FROM json_each(COALESCE(facts.sources, '[]')) AS candidate
              WHERE json_extract(candidate.value, '$.url')
                = json_extract(old_source.value, '$.url')
            )
          UNION ALL
          SELECT new_source.value AS source_json, 10000 + new_source.key AS position
          FROM json_each(
            json_extract((SELECT payload FROM causal_seed), '$.sources')
          ) AS new_source
          WHERE NOT EXISTS (
            SELECT 1
            FROM json_each(COALESCE(facts.sources, '[]')) AS existing
            WHERE json_extract(existing.value, '$.url')
              = json_extract(new_source.value, '$.url')
          )
        )
        ORDER BY position
      )
    )
    ELSE facts.sources
  END
  ,
  updated_at = '2026-08-03'
WHERE facts.chain = json_extract((SELECT payload FROM causal_seed), '$.chain')
  AND facts.dimension IN ('synthesis', '_meta');
