blecoin supply by chain API","publisher":"DefiLlama","url":"https://stablecoins.llama.fi/stablecoinchains","source_role":"independent-data","checked_at":"2026-08-03"}],"forensic_analysis":{"version":"forensic-analysis-v1","observation_snapshot":{"observed_at":"2026-08-03T17:23:37.879Z","observation_completed_at":"2026-08-03T17:23:58.550Z","provider":"DefiLlama","method":"One sequential client run: stablecoinchains once, then the latest historicalChainTvl point and provider-reported 24-hour and 30-day fee, revenue, and DEX aggregates for each chain. Currency aggregates were rounded to cents. Same-day historical points can be revised after this retrieval window.","historical_tvl_point_date":"2026-08-03","latest_tvl_usd":60291854,"peak_tvl_usd":778170342,"peak_tvl_date":"2024-07-21","tvl_drawdown_pct":92.25,"stablecoin_supply":{"pegged_usd":790604508.0084403},"dex_volume_24h_usd":3073385.77,"dex_volume_30d_usd":143323945.54,"fees_24h_usd":119703.02,"fees_30d_usd":27138412.52,"revenue_24h_usd":20936.01,"revenue_30d_usd":1173811.64},"outcome":{"label":"middling","summary":"TON is a high-distribution but middling L1: Telegram access and stablecoin circulation are substantial, while capital depth and reliability remain weaker than the reach implies. In the DefiLlama snapshot begun at 2026-08-03T17:23:37.879Z, the latest 2026-08-03 point was $60.3 million TVL, 92.3% below the July 2024 peak, alongside $790.6 million of dollar stablecoins, $143.3 million of 30-day DEX volume, $27.1 million of fees, and $1.17 million of retained revenue. Two reported outages in August 2024 and the earlier inscription-load incident keep the distribution advantage from supporting a stronger reliability call. These figures are a point-in-time snapshot.","confidence":"high","as_of":"2026-08-03","source_refs":["ton-tvl","ton-stables","ton-volume","ton-fees","ton-revenue","ton-outage"]},"why":{"summary":"Observed: TON carries a stablecoin base far larger than its DeFi TVL and produces material gross fees, yet TVL remains more than ninety percent below peak and retained revenue is a small share of those fees. Documented design: the community continued Telegram''s custom asynchronous architecture after Telegram withdrew, then leaned into wallet, mini-app, payment, and digital-asset distribution inside Telegram rather than EVM portability. Strategic inference: that channel can create consumer transactions and stablecoin movement without first building deep DeFi collateral, explaining why conventional TVL understates reach but not proving durable token value capture. The same choices concentrate platform dependency and custom-tooling risk. The 2023 load report and two 2024 outages show that distribution spikes can exceed validator readiness; they test the architecture but do not establish that Telegram integration caused every reliability failure.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["ton-foundations","ton-primer","ton-inscriptions","ton-outage","ton-tvl","ton-stables","ton-fees","ton-revenue"]},"strategic_choices":[{"decision":"Continue the original TON architecture as a community network after Telegram stopped development.","consequence":"The chain preserved differentiated code and an existing community instead of restarting, but inherited naming, governance, and regulatory-history confusion from the abandoned issuer-led project.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["ton-primer","ton-sec"]},{"decision":"Use a custom asynchronous, dynamically sharded execution model rather than EVM compatibility.","consequence":"TON can optimize for high-throughput consumer services, while developers, validators, and tooling must handle unfamiliar execution and cross-shard behavior.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["ton-foundations","ton-inscriptions"]},{"decision":"Make Telegram the primary wallet, mini-app, and payment distribution surface.","consequence":"Applications can reach a very large existing audience, but policy, product, and integration decisions outside base-layer governance become systemic dependencies.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["ton-primer","ton-stables","ton-fees"]},{"decision":"Prioritize rapid consumer launches and open validator participation while addressing capacity limits through software and operator upgrades.","consequence":"Demand can be admitted quickly, but the inscription report and later outages show that slow operator coordination can turn load events into prolonged liveness failures.","confidence":"high","source_refs":["ton-inscriptions","ton-outage"]}],"counterfactual":{"summary":"An EVM-compatible relaunch might have reduced developer friction and imported more DeFi liquidity, but it would have discarded the custom actor and sharding model and still would not guarantee Telegram distribution. A slower, capacity-gated rollout could have reduced the probability of load-driven stalls, at the cost of limiting the consumer experiments that produced TON''s distinct adoption channel. The evidence supports those trade-offs, not a claim that either alternative would have retained more capital or avoided every outage.","confidence":"medium","source_refs":["ton-foundations","ton-primer","ton-inscriptions","ton-outage","ton-tvl"]},"watch":[{"signal":"Stablecoin supply, DeFi TVL, DEX volume, gross fees, and retained revenue tracked separately.","implication":"Broad improvement would show Telegram distribution converting into a deeper economy; stablecoins and fees growing alone would leave capital depth and value capture unresolved.","source_refs":["ton-stables","ton-tvl","ton-volume","ton-fees","ton-revenue"]},{"signal":"Public incident reports, block-production interruptions, and validator upgrade participation.","implication":"Fewer stalls under comparable demand would support the scaling thesis; repeated coordination failures would keep reliability as the binding constraint.","source_refs":["ton-inscriptions","ton-outage"]},{"signal":"Material changes to Telegram integration, wallet defaults, or TON Foundation governance.","implication":"Deeper integration strengthens distribution but increases correlated platform risk; weaker integration tests whether demand survives without privileged placement.","source_refs":["ton-primer","ton-foundations"]}],"unknowns":[{"question":"What share of current fees comes from durable user payments versus episodic token, game, or airdrop activity?","resolution_trigger":"A reproducible transaction-level fee decomposition covering at least two quarters."},{"question":"How concentrated are validator stake, hosting, and emergency-upgrade coordination today?","resolution_trigger":"A current independent entity-level validator and infrastructure map."},{"question":"What contractual or technical commitments govern Telegram''s long-term TON distribution?","resolution_trigger":"Public durable integration terms or a documented platform-governance commitment."},{"question":"How much stablecoin supply is actively transferred or deployed rather than idle in a small number of accounts?","resolution_trigger":"Independent holder, transfer, and application-concentration analysis."}],"review":{"status":"current","last_reviewed_at":"2026-08-03","next_review_at":"2026-08-10","reviewer":"chaindump-research-desk"}}}')
